RangeForce: Windows Active Directory Reconnaissance – Methods
Platform: RangeForce
Module: Windows Active Directory Reconnaissance – Methods
Difficulty: Beginner / Intermediate
Date: 2026
Objective
The goal of this module was to understand common reconnaissance methods used to gather information from a Windows domain environment running Microsoft Active Directory. The exercise focused on how attackers and analysts enumerate users, groups, and domain information to better understand the network structure.
Tools Used
PowerShell – Used to query domain information and enumerate Active Directory objects.
Mimikatz – Used to demonstrate how credentials and authentication data can be accessed during reconnaissance or post-exploitation.
Key Activities
Used PowerShell commands to enumerate Active Directory users and groups.
Gathered domain information to understand the structure of the environment.
Observed how tools like Mimikatz can extract authentication information from memory.
Identified how reconnaissance techniques can reveal privileged accounts and high-value targets.
Key Takeaways
Active Directory reconnaissance is often performed using native tools like PowerShell, making it difficult to detect.
Tools such as Mimikatz highlight the risk of credential exposure within compromised systems.
Monitoring PowerShell activity and credential access is critical for detecting reconnaissance and post-exploitation behavior.
Outcome:
Completed the module and gained hands-on experience with reconnaissance methods used in Active Directory environments and how defenders can detect suspicious enumeration and credential access activity.
